I realized I was looking for was

00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:18.180
a system for organizing my own
knowledge that could act like a

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:21.300
thinking partner, not just an
ever growing pile of notebooks

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:24.300
and readings, but a system for
organizing ideas that could let

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:27.450
thoughts intermingle, that could
accompany my learning, and that

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:31.410
would be valuable and generative
to return to and explore, and

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:34.290
ideally a tool that I could use
in collaboration with others to

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:38.910
share ideas as we learned. So in
the past five years, there have

00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:41.910
been actually an explosion in
popularity of these types of

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:44.730
tools for creating network
systems for organizing and

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:47.310
sharing knowledge. And while
this type of tool isn't always

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:51.180
described using one single term,
I refer to this genre of tool as

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:53.460
hypermedia knowledge
organization tools, and I'll

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:57.930
take a moment to kind of break
that down. So at its most

00:03:57.930 --> 00:04:02.550
simple, hypermedia, the term
hypermedia comes from hypertext,

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:05.880
which refers to text which is
not constrained to be linear and

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:09.210
where the text itself contains
linkages to other text or other

00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:11.850
documents which a user can kind
of follow based on their own

00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:15.750
interest. And then from there
comes hypermedia more broadly,

00:04:16.290 --> 00:04:19.290
which refers to systems where
other media forms like images

00:04:19.290 --> 00:04:23.280
and video can be connected in
similar ways. The most obvious

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:26.160
example of hypertext and
hypermedia is the internet

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:28.950
itself, where you follow
hyperlinks to navigate networks

00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:32.640
of pages and content. And then
when I say knowledge

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:36.150
organization tools, I'm
referring quite broadly to tools

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:39.810
and practices which helped
arrange and document ideas and

00:04:39.810 --> 00:04:43.410
concepts. So taken together
hypermedia knowledge

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:47.040
organization tools refers to
tools which allow users to

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:49.980
document organize and develop
ideas which employ these

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:54.270
practices of linking and network
forming to to form these like

00:04:54.300 --> 00:05:00.870
networks of interconnected
thoughts. So Many of these

00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:03.930
recent tools promised to
positively affect how people

00:05:03.930 --> 00:05:08.940
think, learn and generate ideas.
These new tools make claims for

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:12.960
helping people create a second
brain in this example here, by

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:14.970
creating and following
connections between notes and

00:05:14.970 --> 00:05:19.590
ideas. they emphasize nonlinear
note taking and knowledge

00:05:19.590 --> 00:05:23.160
organizing, and typically employ
imagery as well as user

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:26.070
interfaces that incorporate
these interconnected nodes of

00:05:26.070 --> 00:05:28.770
ideas. which you can see here.
This is a tool called Rome

00:05:28.770 --> 00:05:33.300
research. Furthermore, they
emphasize collaboration and

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:36.150
connection making across
contexts is another tool called

00:05:36.150 --> 00:05:40.110
arena where you can locate
single ideas in multiple folders

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:44.070
at once. And then finally, many
of these tools propose that

00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:47.520
thinking and learning happen in
these shared organizing spaces.

00:05:48.450 --> 00:05:52.440
So while these recent tools make
pretty lofty claims about their

00:05:52.470 --> 00:05:55.710
new features to help create
context for improved thinking

00:05:55.710 --> 00:05:59.070
and learning, they build upon
both technology and pedagogy

00:05:59.070 --> 00:06:01.410
that has been developing since
the early days of personal

00:06:01.410 --> 00:06:06.240
computers. Despite this history,
and its links to these tools,

00:06:06.270 --> 00:06:09.300
and enter learning itself, we
see little transformation of the

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:13.260
classroom as connected to these
types of tools today. Why is

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:17.520
this and can or should we seek
to change that? So in this in

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:21.420
this thesis, I asked, How can
classrooms use emerging systems

00:06:21.420 --> 00:06:24.060
for collaboratively sharing and
organizing knowledge in order to

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:27.120
benefit learners? And how can we
learn from the past in order to

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:31.380
better shape the future of this
work? I argue that examining the

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:34.440
history of hypermedia oriented
learning tools by looking at

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:37.410
both successful and failed
experiments and bring them to

00:06:37.410 --> 00:06:41.040
classrooms, one can more deeply
understand both the conceptual

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:43.890
origins of these recent
networked knowledge tools, and

00:06:43.890 --> 00:06:45.780
consider where they might be
able to fit into today's

00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:48.510
classrooms, as well as how to
avoid challenges that have

00:06:48.510 --> 00:06:49.500
plagued them in the past.

00:06:52.150 --> 00:06:54.940
So indeed, there's a whole
lineage of these types of

00:06:54.970 --> 00:06:57.670
interactive and collaborative
tools for organizing knowledge

00:06:57.670 --> 00:07:00.460
as networks that have been
experimented with in classrooms.

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:05.110
Since the 1980s. In this thesis,
I look at two previous moments

00:07:05.110 --> 00:07:09.220
of intense development in this
space, one in the mid 1980s, and

00:07:09.220 --> 00:07:12.100
another in the mid 2000s. Before
then turning to examine these

00:07:12.100 --> 00:07:15.400
tools in the present moment. At
each of these points, I look at

00:07:15.430 --> 00:07:18.700
technological shifts, as well as
trends in the learning sciences,

00:07:18.700 --> 00:07:21.820
which together inform both the
design and application of these

00:07:21.820 --> 00:07:26.080
tools. In doing so, I examine
and draw lessons from the

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:28.540
frictions involved in using
these tools in educational

00:07:28.540 --> 00:07:31.780
contexts, in order to consider
and propose ways in which these

00:07:31.780 --> 00:07:35.140
new tools might be able to be
used to support learning in

00:07:35.140 --> 00:07:40.540
classrooms today. As I do so, I
look at emulations and technical

00:07:40.540 --> 00:07:44.110
documentation, older software, I
look at advertisements,

00:07:44.110 --> 00:07:46.990
promotional material and video
content of people using these

00:07:46.990 --> 00:07:51.520
tools. Then Then I serve a
pretty wide variety of research

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:54.130
studies describing the
application of hypermedia

00:07:54.130 --> 00:07:57.340
learning tools and learning
contexts from each of these

00:07:57.340 --> 00:08:01.270
points. So in this presentation,
today, I'm going to do kind of

00:08:01.270 --> 00:08:06.460
two things. First is give sort
of a quick historical overview

00:08:06.460 --> 00:08:08.710
of the development of these
tools and the connections

00:08:08.710 --> 00:08:12.010
between hypermedia and learning
as they've developed and discuss

00:08:12.010 --> 00:08:15.520
some of the frictions between
educational media hypermedia and

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:18.220
classrooms along the way. And
then I'll shift gears and

00:08:18.220 --> 00:08:20.920
describe the current generation
of these tools and point to

00:08:20.920 --> 00:08:23.680
three approaches. But these
types of tools could be used in

00:08:23.680 --> 00:08:27.130
classrooms, one of which I
implemented in a middle school

00:08:27.160 --> 00:08:32.170
classroom context in the fall.
But before getting there, I want

00:08:32.170 --> 00:08:36.820
to touch on why why this area is
important. Firstly, building new

00:08:36.820 --> 00:08:40.480
capacities to document organize
and structure knowledge is a

00:08:40.480 --> 00:08:43.390
crucial 21st century skill, and
one that becomes ever more

00:08:43.390 --> 00:08:45.910
relevant when encountering
increasing information,

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:50.020
information saturation that we
all deal with today. However,

00:08:50.470 --> 00:08:53.470
making changes to education
broadly, is an incredibly

00:08:53.470 --> 00:08:56.560
challenging task. And
particularly in the context of

00:08:56.560 --> 00:08:59.620
educational technology, new
tools, with great promise come

00:08:59.620 --> 00:09:02.650
in and out of popularity quite
regularly, and very rarely make

00:09:02.650 --> 00:09:05.470
any significant changes in
popular educational discourse.

00:09:06.790 --> 00:09:10.240
In his book, failure to disrupt
why technology alone can't

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:13.180
transform education, Justin
Reich talks in depth about why

00:09:13.180 --> 00:09:15.490
so many of the technologies
which have been promised will

00:09:15.490 --> 00:09:19.060
radically transform educational
practices have failed. He sets

00:09:19.060 --> 00:09:22.390
forth the juxtaposition between
two stances, the charismatic

00:09:22.390 --> 00:09:26.110
stance and the tinkering stance.
The charismatic stance typically

00:09:26.110 --> 00:09:29.080
accompanies technological
innovation, the notion that

00:09:29.170 --> 00:09:31.780
technology itself inherently
will offer wholesale

00:09:31.780 --> 00:09:34.300
transformation of existing
systems, and it's this stance

00:09:34.300 --> 00:09:39.640
that often fails. In comparison,
the tinkering stance, he says,

00:09:39.760 --> 00:09:42.910
sees schools and universities as
complex systems which can be

00:09:42.910 --> 00:09:46.000
improved, but they believe that
major improvement is the product

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:48.940
of many years of incremental
changes to existing institutions

00:09:48.940 --> 00:09:52.810
rather than the result of one
stroke of wholesale renewal. So

00:09:52.810 --> 00:09:55.660
with this thesis, I hope to take
a tinkerer stance on considering

00:09:55.870 --> 00:09:58.750
the possible value of these
types of tools. I see this and

00:09:58.750 --> 00:10:01.300
trying to pull lessons from the
Earlier errors of hypermedia

00:10:01.300 --> 00:10:04.510
learning that I examine and
consider how these how these

00:10:04.510 --> 00:10:07.450
tools might be able to offer
some incremental changes to

00:10:07.450 --> 00:10:12.640
classrooms. So first I'm going
to talk a little bit about some

00:10:12.640 --> 00:10:16.600
of the origins of the connection
between hypermedia and, and

00:10:16.600 --> 00:10:20.440
learning. So any account of the
history of hypertext and

00:10:20.440 --> 00:10:24.220
hypermedia tends to begin with
Vannevar Bush. He was an

00:10:24.220 --> 00:10:27.460
American engineer and inventor,
and led the US Office of

00:10:27.460 --> 00:10:29.350
Scientific research and
development throughout World War

00:10:29.350 --> 00:10:35.230
Two. Following the war, Bush
sought to imagine schools as

00:10:35.230 --> 00:10:38.350
sorry, imagine tools, which
might be able to enable the

00:10:38.350 --> 00:10:42.280
human mind to more readily think
and learn across disciplines. In

00:10:42.280 --> 00:10:44.500
part this stemmed from a fear
that the increasing hyper

00:10:44.500 --> 00:10:46.750
specialization which he saw
amongst his research

00:10:46.750 --> 00:10:49.150
organizations would lead to a
mountain of inaccessible

00:10:49.150 --> 00:10:53.020
knowledge. It is 1945 piece in
The Atlantic titled as we may

00:10:53.020 --> 00:10:55.930
think, Bush proposed a device
called the memex. To deal with

00:10:55.930 --> 00:10:59.710
this challenge. The memex was a
device which would hold

00:10:59.710 --> 00:11:02.710
microfilm copies of a user's
personal library, and allow them

00:11:02.710 --> 00:11:05.710
to make automatic connections
between text such that they

00:11:05.710 --> 00:11:09.610
could create chains of
intertextual Association. Users

00:11:09.610 --> 00:11:13.120
of the memex device would save
and share these paths of ideas

00:11:13.120 --> 00:11:16.660
between texts as well as their
notes about them. As is apparent

00:11:16.660 --> 00:11:20.260
in the title of this piece, Bush
wanting to develop a device that

00:11:20.260 --> 00:11:22.600
he operates as he believed, we
may think.

00:11:23.890 --> 00:11:26.050
This notion that the mind
naturally operates by

00:11:26.050 --> 00:11:28.810
association carries throughout
this entire text. As he says,

00:11:28.990 --> 00:11:32.110
with one item in its grasp, the
brain instantly snaps to the

00:11:32.110 --> 00:11:35.020
next that is suggested by the
Association of thoughts in

00:11:35.020 --> 00:11:37.510
accordance with some intricate
web of trails carried by the

00:11:37.510 --> 00:11:41.860
cells of the brain. So embedded
in his writing are few notions

00:11:41.860 --> 00:11:44.800
about learning that carry across
many of the hypermedia tools for

00:11:44.800 --> 00:11:46.930
organizing knowledge that
developed in the following

00:11:46.930 --> 00:11:51.520
years. First is the idea that
the mind naturally thinks and

00:11:51.520 --> 00:11:55.030
learns by making these
associations. The second is that

00:11:55.180 --> 00:11:58.150
associations between ideas can
link together from one to the

00:11:58.150 --> 00:12:00.550
next to form trails of
connection as Bush describes,

00:12:01.360 --> 00:12:04.150
developing tools by which one
can create and save these trails

00:12:04.150 --> 00:12:07.390
will allow for deeper learning.
And then third is that these

00:12:07.390 --> 00:12:09.610
trails are meaningful chunks of
knowledge which can be built

00:12:09.610 --> 00:12:12.670
collaboratively, where sharing
and co creating networks of

00:12:12.670 --> 00:12:15.880
association between ideas will
inherently lead to deeper

00:12:15.880 --> 00:12:20.590
learning. And then, in the 20.
In the 20 years that followed,

00:12:21.130 --> 00:12:24.190
the terms hypertext and
hypermedia themselves were later

00:12:24.190 --> 00:12:28.150
coined by Ted Nelson in the
1960s, who imagined digital

00:12:28.150 --> 00:12:31.570
systems which would use these
models. He created diagrams to

00:12:31.600 --> 00:12:34.330
illustrate the idea of hypertext
and hypermedia, one of which you

00:12:34.330 --> 00:12:39.040
can see here and this this
imagery is often repeated

00:12:39.250 --> 00:12:43.600
throughout the types of tools
that we look at today. So

00:12:45.750 --> 00:12:46.650
Unknown: okay, yes.

00:12:48.160 --> 00:12:50.290
Will Freudenheim: Though this
optimism in the power of

00:12:50.290 --> 00:12:53.740
hypermedia to accelerate
learning remained semi dormant

00:12:53.740 --> 00:12:55.960
for a few decades in terms of
tools that were actually

00:12:55.960 --> 00:13:00.160
developed. By the mid 1980s.
Multiple hypermedia platforms

00:13:00.160 --> 00:13:03.670
simultaneously sprung forth that
were specifically designed for

00:13:03.670 --> 00:13:07.120
the educational context.
Researchers from across

00:13:07.120 --> 00:13:09.610
educational and commercial
institutions promised that

00:13:09.610 --> 00:13:12.580
hypermedia oriented learning
tools would offer learners newer

00:13:12.580 --> 00:13:15.130
and faster ways of accessing
knowledge, new modes of

00:13:15.130 --> 00:13:18.220
collaboration, and new types of
interaction to enable learners

00:13:18.220 --> 00:13:22.480
to forge connections between
ideas. By the mid 20th century,

00:13:22.600 --> 00:13:25.240
new theories of knowledge
acquisition began to describe

00:13:25.240 --> 00:13:28.270
learning and thinking as
processes using computational

00:13:28.270 --> 00:13:31.030
and diagrammatic metaphors,
which sounded much like Ted

00:13:31.030 --> 00:13:33.970
Nelson and Vannevar Bush's early
descriptions of hypertext

00:13:33.970 --> 00:13:36.940
systems. These perspectives on
learning were foundational in

00:13:36.940 --> 00:13:39.310
bridging the language of
hypertext to the language of

00:13:39.310 --> 00:13:42.310
learning, which deeply informed
the educational hypermedia tools

00:13:42.310 --> 00:13:46.600
to come. So I'm going to talk
about two of them briefly. One

00:13:46.600 --> 00:13:48.490
model of learning that stands
out for its overlap with

00:13:48.490 --> 00:13:51.880
hypertext vocabulary is the
model of schemas, a concept from

00:13:51.880 --> 00:13:54.400
psychology and cognitive science
which describes patterns of

00:13:54.400 --> 00:13:56.920
thought or behavior which
organize information and the

00:13:56.920 --> 00:14:01.030
relationships between them. The
model of schema, considers

00:14:01.030 --> 00:14:03.820
mental constructs as individual
nodes which together form

00:14:03.820 --> 00:14:06.550
networks of ideas through
associative structures, which

00:14:06.550 --> 00:14:09.070
consist of attributes and
relationships between them.

00:14:09.760 --> 00:14:13.570
schemas, focus attention on the
role that old knowledge plays an

00:14:13.570 --> 00:14:16.450
inquiry in acquiring new
knowledge in the sense that new

00:14:16.450 --> 00:14:19.480
knowledge is filtered and
interpreted through these webs

00:14:19.480 --> 00:14:23.890
of pre existing associations.
Another important model to

00:14:23.890 --> 00:14:25.990
understand learning that was
growing in popularity at this

00:14:25.990 --> 00:14:29.800
time was the idea of social
constructivism. In the

00:14:29.800 --> 00:14:32.620
educational context, social
