link. They prioritize creating
and connecting smaller nodes of

00:29:03.840 --> 00:29:07.410
information. So while with the
wiki where the page was kind of

00:29:07.410 --> 00:29:10.530
the individual node that could
connect to other pages, many of

00:29:10.530 --> 00:29:12.660
these contemporary tools
prioritize creating and

00:29:12.660 --> 00:29:15.030
connecting smaller chunks of
information together like

00:29:15.060 --> 00:29:19.440
individual bullet points, or
individual images. And then

00:29:19.440 --> 00:29:22.710
finally, many of these emerging
platforms offer multiple ways of

00:29:22.710 --> 00:29:25.320
creating and viewing the
connections between information

00:29:25.350 --> 00:29:28.650
and their relationships as well,
so often employing graph views

00:29:28.650 --> 00:29:34.140
to kind of visualize nodes of
interconnection. So the final

00:29:34.140 --> 00:29:38.340
section of my thesis then asks,
Where might these ideas and

00:29:38.340 --> 00:29:41.100
platforms fit into classrooms
today? And how can we learn from

00:29:41.100 --> 00:29:45.360
some of these frictions of
previous tools? So I identify

00:29:45.360 --> 00:29:48.210
three possible ways that I see
these contemporary tools being

00:29:48.210 --> 00:29:53.040
used. that avoids some of the
information frictions. And each

00:29:53.040 --> 00:29:56.280
of these models are just
inspired by practices that I've

00:29:56.550 --> 00:30:01.890
seen people using online. The
first is the concept of creating

00:30:01.920 --> 00:30:06.060
a digital garden. So digital
gardens are online spaces that

00:30:06.060 --> 00:30:08.490
looks something like a
combination of a notebook and a

00:30:08.490 --> 00:30:12.510
blog. Digital gardeners write
essays or short form pieces and

00:30:12.510 --> 00:30:16.170
use backlinks at the end of each
page to point to other pieces

00:30:16.170 --> 00:30:20.340
inside their garden to connect
to the piece that the reader is

00:30:20.340 --> 00:30:24.510
currently reading to others,
it's like a kind of personal

00:30:24.540 --> 00:30:27.480
website or a blog with the added
notion that the written pieces

00:30:27.480 --> 00:30:31.530
are intentionally interlinked
together. And in addition, this

00:30:31.530 --> 00:30:34.050
kind of garden metaphor implies
that the connections on each

00:30:34.050 --> 00:30:37.920
page as well as the content
itself, will be tended to, and

00:30:37.920 --> 00:30:40.740
that the posts themselves aren't
meant to be final but can grow

00:30:40.740 --> 00:30:45.360
later on. Digital gardens are a
model that could be useful in a

00:30:45.360 --> 00:30:49.710
variety of contexts. But for an
example, a middle school age

00:30:49.710 --> 00:30:52.110
learner and an English class
could use a digital garden to

00:30:52.110 --> 00:30:55.410
write not only their homework,
assignments, essays and creative

00:30:55.410 --> 00:30:58.590
work in one shared space, and
could be encouraged to

00:30:58.590 --> 00:31:01.740
explicitly make connections
between these different forms.

00:31:02.970 --> 00:31:05.580
This model is developed by an
individual rather than a group,

00:31:05.910 --> 00:31:09.210
and is more focused on
organizing and sharing work that

00:31:09.210 --> 00:31:12.240
a learner has themselves created
rather than inputting knowledge

00:31:12.240 --> 00:31:15.330
created by others. So this has
the benefit of giving teachers a

00:31:15.330 --> 00:31:18.300
space with which to evaluate the
student's work as they use the

00:31:18.300 --> 00:31:26.190
tool. A second approach is using
these shared knowledge

00:31:26.190 --> 00:31:28.290
organization tools for creating
collaborative research

00:31:28.290 --> 00:31:32.040
repositories. So using these
tools, learners that are part of

00:31:32.040 --> 00:31:36.510
a class or group of classes can
collectively gather, organize

00:31:36.510 --> 00:31:39.870
and annotate external materials
that will be useful for research

00:31:39.870 --> 00:31:43.440
and writing that will be
developed separately. This is a

00:31:43.440 --> 00:31:46.920
type of practice that I've seen
on one of the tools called arena

00:31:47.640 --> 00:31:51.630
that I examined previously. This
type of model will allow

00:31:51.630 --> 00:31:54.870
students to help each other
gather materials that will be

00:31:54.870 --> 00:31:58.050
useful for their own individual
work, and in doing so builds a

00:31:58.050 --> 00:32:00.870
shared system that is
potentially useful to the entire

00:32:00.870 --> 00:32:04.230
class as a whole. And while this
model is inherently

00:32:04.230 --> 00:32:07.710
collaborative, it's in a kind of
prewriting context that avoids

00:32:07.710 --> 00:32:10.470
the challenges of needing to
figure out how to grade

00:32:10.470 --> 00:32:12.300
individual students on their
contributions.

00:32:14.460 --> 00:32:17.670
And then the third model that I
talked about, which is what I

00:32:17.670 --> 00:32:21.630
explore in a little bit more
depth in a case study is using

00:32:21.630 --> 00:32:24.360
network tools to support social
emotional learning in

00:32:24.360 --> 00:32:27.570
classrooms. This model is
inspired by a project that I

00:32:27.570 --> 00:32:30.960
worked on with the playful
journey lab at MIT. In

00:32:30.960 --> 00:32:34.770
collaboration with a seventh
grade, Massachusetts area public

00:32:35.220 --> 00:32:39.300
middle school classroom in the
fall, we used a tool called

00:32:39.300 --> 00:32:42.120
brown research, which is one of
the contemporary tools that I

00:32:42.300 --> 00:32:45.780
like over in the thesis to host
an activity series format that

00:32:45.780 --> 00:32:48.960
focused on collaborative
reflection, in order to support

00:32:49.380 --> 00:32:51.900
students and teachers to
acknowledge a kind of wide

00:32:51.900 --> 00:32:54.810
variety of experiences and types
of learning that that happened

00:32:54.810 --> 00:32:58.590
throughout the pandemic. The
types of prompts that we

00:32:58.590 --> 00:33:02.730
designed, involved virtual
making and collaborative

00:33:02.730 --> 00:33:08.040
writing, that that is also
incorporated like drawing simple

00:33:08.040 --> 00:33:11.190
video making and taking
pictures. The intention was that

00:33:11.190 --> 00:33:14.520
students would come away from
this activity series with this

00:33:15.270 --> 00:33:19.230
co created collection of shared
artifacts that captured this

00:33:19.230 --> 00:33:22.830
moment in their lives. That led
to like deeper discussion. And

00:33:22.830 --> 00:33:26.310
that helped expand their ideas
of what learning is. And in

00:33:26.310 --> 00:33:30.240
doing so, they worked in this
kind of shared knowledge

00:33:30.240 --> 00:33:36.120
environment over the course of a
semester. Using using the to

00:33:36.120 --> 00:33:39.750
these tools in this context is,
again, kind of outside of a

00:33:39.750 --> 00:33:42.630
context that requires grading,
and also further further

00:33:42.630 --> 00:33:46.170
encourages collaboration and
connection forming. And then

00:33:46.200 --> 00:33:49.590
also, using using these tools
for a shared social emotional

00:33:49.590 --> 00:33:54.780
space, could be a way to help
learners in distance learning

00:33:54.780 --> 00:33:59.340
environments as well. Also also
developing an activity series in

00:33:59.340 --> 00:34:02.160
this using this format, is a way
of avoiding some of the

00:34:02.160 --> 00:34:05.580
procrastination problems that
Wikipedia or the sorry that

00:34:05.580 --> 00:34:09.720
Wikis often dealt with. I could
talk a lot more about this

00:34:09.750 --> 00:34:12.960
specific project. But I'm going
to kind of start to start to

00:34:12.960 --> 00:34:18.660
wrap up. So looking at both the
history and present moment of

00:34:18.840 --> 00:34:22.470
network tools in the context of
learning. My hope today is to

00:34:22.470 --> 00:34:25.650
kind of leave you with a gentle
optimism about their potential.

00:34:26.790 --> 00:34:29.730
Well, I don't see these tools on
their own as radically are

00:34:29.730 --> 00:34:32.730
fundamentally transforming
educational practices, I see

00:34:32.730 --> 00:34:35.580
that there clearly is a design
space that can be continued to

00:34:35.580 --> 00:34:39.600
be explored using these types of
tools to support learners in a

00:34:39.600 --> 00:34:43.110
variety of different contexts.
So I think that taking a

00:34:43.110 --> 00:34:46.440
tinkerers approach to looking at
the past configurations of the

00:34:46.440 --> 00:34:50.100
genre of tool and carefully
examining the contexts within

00:34:50.100 --> 00:34:53.070
which they can be employed is
essential to using them to

00:34:53.070 --> 00:34:56.700
create change. And then
ultimately, what what is more

00:34:56.700 --> 00:34:59.850
important, most important is
less the tools themselves but

00:35:00.390 --> 00:35:02.850
careful consideration of how
they fit into the environments

00:35:02.850 --> 00:35:07.590
in which we use them. So thank
you all so much. Thank you to

